1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,752
Go has no try catch.

2
00:00:01,972 --> 00:00:03,432
So how do errors work?

3
00:00:03,652 --> 00:00:04,927
This is Go Part

4
00:00:04,927 --> 00:00:06,712
28, errors as values.

5
00:00:07,432 --> 00:00:09,473
The function returns two things,

6
00:00:09,473 --> 00:00:10,876
a result and an error.

7
00:00:11,096 --> 00:00:12,580
If something goes wrong,

8
00:00:12,580 --> 00:00:13,940
return errors dot New.

9
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:15,049
If all is fine,

10
00:00:15,049 --> 00:00:16,057
the error is nil.

11
00:00:16,777 --> 00:00:18,039
Ten divided by two

12
00:00:18,039 --> 00:00:19,373
gives five, and the

13
00:00:19,373 --> 00:00:20,285
error is nil.

14
00:00:20,505 --> 00:00:22,365
Ten divided by zero gives

15
00:00:22,365 --> 00:00:23,779
an error, so if err

16
00:00:23,779 --> 00:00:25,267
is not nil, we print

17
00:00:25,267 --> 00:00:26,160
it and stop.

18
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:28,081
Why do it this way?

19
00:00:28,301 --> 00:00:29,110
Every error is

20
00:00:29,110 --> 00:00:29,977
in plain sight.

21
00:00:30,197 --> 00:00:31,619
Nothing jumps away, and

22
00:00:31,619 --> 00:00:32,793
you handle it right

23
00:00:32,793 --> 00:00:33,844
where it happens.

24
00:00:34,564 --> 00:00:36,150
Next time, custom errors

25
00:00:36,150 --> 00:00:37,339
and errors dot Is.

